  <header class="lore-header">
    <p class="lore-pillar">Norse Lore · NOR·04</p>
    <h1>Ravens in the Prose Edda</h1>
    <p class="lore-subtitle">Snorri Sturluson's 13th-Century Account</p>
  </header>

  <section class="lore-snippet" aria-label="Quick answer">
    <p>Snorri Sturluson's <em>Prose Edda</em> (c. 1220 CE) contains the most detailed account of Huginn and Muninn in <em>Gylfaginning</em> chapter 38, where it names them, describes their daily flight, and calls Odin <em>Hrafnaguð</em> ("Raven-God"). A second section, <em>Skáldskaparmál</em>, catalogs raven kennings used by Viking Age skalds.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section">
    <h2>What the Prose Edda Is — and Is Not</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The <em>Prose Edda</em> was written around 1220 CE by Snorri Sturluson (1179–1241 CE), a Christian Icelandic politician and scholar. It has three main sections: <em>Gylfaginning</em> (mythological narrative), <em>Skáldskaparmál</em> (poetic diction), and <em>Háttatal</em> (metre). Snorri wrote as a literary antiquarian — preserving knowledge of Old Norse poetic conventions and myths that were falling out of active use as Iceland became Christian.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> This matters for ravens: Snorri's account of Huginn and Muninn is fuller than anything in the <em>Poetic Edda</em>, but he was writing roughly 200 years after the Viking Age. His systematisation of myth may reflect genuine oral tradition, his own interpretation, or both. Modern scholars — including Anthony Faulkes, whose 1987 edition remains the standard — treat him as a secondary source that must be checked against the older Eddic poems.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section">
    <h2>Gylfaginning Chapter 38 — the Full Raven Passage</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The relevant passage from <em>Gylfaginning</em> ch. 38 (paraphrase following Faulkes 1987, p. 33):</p>
    <blockquote class="lore-quote">
      <p>Odin has two ravens which sit on his shoulders. The one is called Huginn, the other Muninn. They fly out at daybreak and come back at meal-time. Thus he gets information about all kinds of things. Hence he is called Raven-God (<em>Hrafnaguð</em>). As it says in <em>Grímnismál</em>: [stanza 20 follows].</p>
      <footer>— Paraphrase of <em>Gylfaginning</em> ch. 38, Snorri Sturluson, c. 1220 CE<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></footer>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Snorri's additions beyond <em>Grímnismál</em> stanza 20 are:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The ravens sit on Odin's shoulders (not stated in the Poetic Edda).</li>
      <li>They depart at daybreak and return at meal-time (a specific daily schedule).</li>
      <li>The purpose is for Odin to get "news of all things" (<em>tíðendi af öllum löndum</em>).</li>
      <li>The by-name <em>Hrafnaguð</em> is explicitly linked to this behaviour.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section">
    <h2>Skáldskaparmál — Raven Kennings</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> <em>Skáldskaparmál</em> ("The Language of Poetry") is a catalogue of poetic diction. It lists kennings — circumlocutory compound phrases — used in skaldic verse. Ravens appear extensively as a kenning element for corpses, warriors, and Odin.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>Examples of raven kennings from <em>Skáldskaparmál</em> (Faulkes 1987 edition):</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>Huginn</em> used as a heiti for "raven" in kennings for a warrior (the "feeder of Huginn").</li>
      <li>The slain described as "food of ravens" (<em>hrafnfóðr</em>).</li>
      <li>"Raven's wine" (<em>hrafns vín</em>) = blood, in a battle kenning.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>These kennings confirm that ravens were a standard element of Viking Age poetic vocabulary around death and battle — reinforcing the connection between Odin's ravens and the battlefield.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section">
    <h2>What Snorri Adds and What He Might Have Invented</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The specific detail that the ravens sit on Odin's shoulders appears in <em>Gylfaginning</em> but not in the <em>Poetic Edda</em>. It is, however, consistent with Migration Period and Viking Age imagery: the Vendel helmet plate finds and the Thorsberg chape both show birds close to or on a central figure. Whether Snorri was drawing on a lost oral tradition, visual art, or his own inference from the shoulder-sitting motif is unknown.<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
    <p>Neil Price notes in <em>The Viking Way</em> (2002) that Snorri is generally most reliable when he is quoting or paraphrasing older poems directly (as he does with <em>Grímnismál</em> stanza 20), and least reliable when providing prose elaborations without poetic support.<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section lore-bridge">
    <h2>The Birds Behind the Story</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Common Ravens do perch on and near large animals and have been recorded landing on the backs of large mammals in the wild. Their habituation to humans and propensity to perch at close range would make Snorri's shoulder-sitting detail plausible to any observer of real ravens.<sup><a href="#src-4">4</a></sup></p>
    <p><a href="/species/common-raven/" class="lore-species-link">Common Raven — species profile →</a></p>
    <p><a href="/lore/norse/huginn-and-muninn/" class="lore-species-link">Huginn and Muninn — primary source analysis →</a></p>
  </section>

